# Tuned JVM flags for the detokenization service (S9.1, S9.2).
#
# The workload shape this is tuned for: detokenization allocates heavily but almost entirely
# short-lived -- request DTOs, Jackson buffers, byte arrays for cryptograms and ciphertext -- with a
# small long-lived set (the near-cache, connection pools). Most garbage dies in eden, which is G1's
# sweet spot. The goal is not throughput; it is keeping any single pause well inside a ~22 ms budget.
#
# Flag by flag, and what an interviewer is likely to push on:
#
#  -Xms = -Xmx            No heap resizing at runtime, so no resize pauses. Also makes the numbers
#                         reproducible: a growing heap changes GC behaviour mid-measurement.
#
#  -Xmx4g (up from 2g)    More headroom means fewer collections, and fewer collections means fewer
#                         chances to produce an outlier. Headroom is not free -- it is memory you
#                         could have spent on the near-cache -- which is why it is a deliberate 2x
#                         rather than "as much as possible".
#
#  MaxGCPauseMillis=15    A GOAL, not a guarantee. G1 sizes the young generation to try to meet it.
#                         Set it aggressively low and G1 shrinks young gen, collects more often, and
#                         throughput drops while pauses barely improve -- the failure mode is real and
#                         worth demonstrating. 15 ms sits under the latency budget with room for the
#                         rest of the request.
#
#  G1HeapRegionSize=8m    Right-sized for the object mix. Too small and the region count explodes,
#                         making remembered-set work expensive; too large and small collections
#                         become coarse.
#
#  IHOP=35                Start concurrent marking earlier than the default. The worst G1 pauses come
#  G1ReservePercent=15    from to-space exhaustion: G1 has nowhere to copy survivors and falls back to
#                         a much longer pause. Marking earlier and holding space in reserve is what
#                         prevents the "everything was fine, then 200 ms" p99.9 spike. This pair is
#                         the actual mechanism behind the tail improvement -- the others are hygiene.
#
#  ParallelRefProcEnabled Reference processing runs in parallel instead of serially inside the pause.
#
#  AlwaysPreTouch         Touch every heap page at start-up. Removes first-hit page-fault jitter,
#                         which otherwise shows up as unexplained early-life latency spikes. Costs a
#                         slower start, which is the right trade for a long-lived service.

JAVA_OPTS="-Xms4g -Xmx4g \
-XX:+UseG1GC \
-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=15 \
-XX:G1HeapRegionSize=8m \
-XX:InitiatingHeapOccupancyPercent=35 \
-XX:G1ReservePercent=15 \
-XX:+ParallelRefProcEnabled \
-XX:+AlwaysPreTouch \
-Xlog:gc*,safepoint:file=/var/log/pts/gc-tuned.log:t,uptime,level,tags"
